+To compare them under the same setting, we use the seeds released by SmartFuzz and run them with \griffin{} on all four target DBMSs, with the same DBMS versions, resource allocation, campaign duration, and coverage measurement as the other tools in RQ2. As shown in Table~III, Table~IV, and Figure~7 below, \tool{} covers 7\% more branches and detects 5 more bugs than SmartFuzz. On Virtuoso, whose dialect differs most from the source DBMSs (the lowest execution validity of \tool{} in Table~V), SmartFuzz achieves slightly higher coverage, likely because it generates seeds directly from the official documentation of the target DBMS.
 
